  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 AM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nToday through Thu...At discussion time, showers and \nthunderstorms were still ongoing in the northern half of the \nforecast area, but by morning should be limited to four northern\ncounties: Custer, Lemhi, Clark, and Fremont. Would expect no \nmore than scattered coverage for showers and isolated conditions\nfor thunderstorms for all day today. By late afternoon \nlocations in the south will have clear skies. This should allow \ncolder temperatures tonight, but the warm-up will overcome this \ncold start as clear skies continue for several days. \n\nThe passing front last night pushed winds to near Wind Advisory\nthis overnight, but wind has subsided already and should not\nincrease again until the next trough makes an approach from the\nwest on Thu afternoon. Limited areas may reach critical levels\nof humidity and gusts that afternoon for wildfire danger.\n\nThu night through Sun...This period starts with a east-west\noriented trough sliding eastward to the north of southern\nIdaho. However, just how far south it digs is considerably\ndifferent in the ensembles. Four clusters result, all with 20-30\npercent chance of occurrence. About 50 percent have the low far\nenough south to bring showers and thunderstorms back to at least\nportions of the forecast area (mainly north) on Sat and Sun. \nThe other 40 percent shows the trough staying far enough north \nto lessen the risk of showers. Will just have to wait and see.",
